  "success": true,
  "output": "I read through the actual source (not the prior automated audit logs I found sitting in `.claude-flow/logs`, though I cross-checked against them after). This is a very early-stage Next.js 16 app — almost entirely static Server Components with a couple of framer-motion entrance animations. That changes the shape of the answer: most of the classic categories don't have live examples yet, so I'm reporting what's actually there plus the two real findings.\n\n## What actually exists right now\n- Routes: `/`, `/get-started`, `/sign-up`, `/(console)/sources` — that's it.\n- Only one server data-access module: `lib/server/sources.ts`, backed by Drizzle + `postgres-js`.\n- Zero `useEffect`/`useState`/`setInterval`/`addEventListener` anywhere in the tree — confirmed via grep across all `.ts`/`.tsx`. No client-side subscriptions exist yet, so there's nothing to leak.\n- `entities`, `matches`, `conflicts`, `runs` tables exist in the schema but have **no query code written against them yet** — so true N+1 patterns (the kind that show up when you fetch matches and then loop to fetch each entity) haven't been introduced. Worth flagging now, before that code gets written.\n\n## 1. N+1 queries — none live, one is coming\nNo loop-per-row query exists today. But `sources.ts:23-30` has the shape that turns into N+1 the moment it's copied for another table:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts — current\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();       // full select + full Zod parse of every row\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nFetch-everything-then-filter-in-JS is fine at 10 sources; it stops being fine once `matches`/`conflicts` need the same treatment (e.g., \"count conflicts by severity\" doing a full-table pull + per-row Zod parse). Push the aggregation into SQL:\n\n```ts\nimport { sql } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const [row] = await getDb()\n    .select({\n      crm: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${sources.kind} in ('salesforce','hubspot'))`,\n      warehouse: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${sources.kind} in ('databricks','snowflake'))`,\n      synthetic: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${sources.kind} = 'synthetic')`,\n    })\n    .from(sources);\n  return row;\n}\n```\n\n**Forward-looking**: when you write the `matches` → `entities` lookup (matches page, conflict detail view), reach for a Drizzle `join`/`innerJoin` on `entityAId`/`entityBId`, not a `Promise.all` of per-match entity fetches. That's the concrete N+1 trap this schema sets up.\n\n## 2. Unnecessary re-renders — not applicable yet\nEvery interactive-looking piece (`LandingHero`, `GetStartedHero`, `StepList`, `SourcesEmptyState`, `PipelineStrip`) is a small \"use client\" wrapper around a static `motion.div`/`motion.li` with a `const` array defined at module scope — no props flowing from a re-rendering parent, no derived state, nothing to memoize. `NavRail` calls `usePathname()` and computes `active` per nav item, but the list is 5 static items — `useMemo` there would be overhead, not a fix. There's genuinely nothing to report here yet; revisit once the console has stateful client components (filters, live run status, table sorting).\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities — the one real gap\n`listSources()` isn't wrapped in React's `cache()`:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts\nimport \"server-only\";\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\nimport { connection } from \"next/server\";\n...\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n\nRight now each page calls it at most once, so there's no live double-fetch — but `cache()` de-dupes calls *within a single render pass*, which is cheap insurance the moment a layout and a page both need the source list (e.g., once the console rail shows a source count badge). Note `connection()` already opts this out of cross-request caching deliberately (per the comment — sources are request-time state), so don't reach for `unstable_cache` here; that would fight the existing design.\n\n`next/font` (`IBM_Plex_Sans`/`IBM_Plex_Mono` in `app/layout.tsx`) is already self-hosted/cached correctly — no action needed.\n\n## 4. Memory leaks — none found\nNo `setInterval`, `setTimeout`, `addEventListener`, or manual subscriptions anywhere in the codebase. The db client in `lib/db/index.ts` correctly caches the Postgres connection on `globalThis` to survive HMR reloads in dev, which is the right pattern (prevents connection-pool exhaustion from repeated dev reloads).\n\n## 5. Redundant computations\nSame `countSourcesBySide` issue as #1 — it Zod-parses every source row (including `config.objects` arrays) just to throw away the parsed data and count kinds. The SQL aggregate fix above eliminates that. Separately, its CRM/warehouse bucketing duplicates the `SOURCE_KIND_SIDE` map already defined in `types/source.ts:44` — worth reusing that map instead of hardcoding the kind list a second place they can drift apart:\n\n```ts\ncrm: all.filter((s) => SOURCE_KIND_SIDE[s.kind] === \"crm\").length,\n```\n\n(if you keep the JS-side version rather than moving to SQL).\n\n---\n\n**One thing worth your attention, unrelated to the code itself**: `.claude-flow/logs/headless/` has 47 automated `audit`/`optimize`/`testgaps` runs, roughly every ~10 minutes, most recent finishing 5 minutes before this session started. That looks like an active scheduled loop-worker still running against this repo — its `optimize_*_result.log` entries already independently flagged the same two `sources.ts` issues I found above. If that loop isn't something you intentionally left running, it's worth checking `ruflo-loop-workers`/`autopilot` config, since it's consuming API calls on a cadence right now.\n",
